A||||1||daved||Detroit Auto Show, Brakes & exotic cars||||1||
Z||000000||daved||01-24-2000||09:07 PM||||Wow!<BR>Anyone else at the Detroit show.  I was there on Saturday, along with 50,000 other auto enthusiasts.  I must have been the only one there sliding under every car with disk rear brakes...see what our addiction...er hobby makes us do!<BR>  I was having a good look at the technical aspects of rear brakes and undercarriage. Undercarriage: not much to report there. But brakes, Ah ha, some differnet things I saw. The old Fiero style of hydraulic was in abundance, though mostly with the mechanicals at right-angles to the brake cylinder; some kind of cam in there? Then the cunning ones on SUV of some kind (they all look the same to me)where the engineers cast a smaller drum brake into the hub, not much larger in diameter than a normal hub,  along with the the usual vented disk. Works, and reliable I would think, but no good to us. Then there was the sports car solution, many with seperate little "lawnmower style" mechnical calipers (a few of them still using solid disks). Best find was on the Lambo. Had the name 'Bimbro' (looked like) cast in the mechanical caliper.  Maybe this would be the rear brake solution for us...I mean the style of mechanical brake, not the manufacturer.  Only a simple mounting bracket would be required.  It's a thought anyway.<BR>  Took lots of pics (no, not the digital kind), and spent some time at the Ferrari compound.  Strangely, no one was allowed near those works of art.  Hmmmm... maybe in another life I could afford at least one wheel!  How about the spec. on these cars;<BR> "0 to 1 mile in 30.4 secs"<BR>Says it all,  Dave||||reg||1
Z||000001||Standard||01-24-2000||11:56 PM||||Are you sure the name on those lambo brakes wasn't Brembo?||||reg||1
